{
  "dom_id": "step-q343321",
  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14187947/assets/baa2d9cf6ae9d34de744374dbfe67e44589aa9331c1391e5fc168cd6a39bca4d.png)\n\nJennie fills an aquarium, in the shape of a rectangular prism that is $50\\,\\text{cm}$ long and $25\\,\\text{cm}$ wide, with water from a tap. How fast is the volume of water in the aquarium changing if the water level is rising at a rate of $0.2\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{h}?$\n\nThe volume of water in the aquarium is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/h}.$",
  "worked_solution": "We define the following variables:\n\n- let $L$ and $W$ be the length and width of the aquarium, respectively, and\n\n- let $h$ and $V$ be the height and volume of the water, respectively.\n\nNote that $h$ and $V$ change with time, while $L$ and $W$ are constants. \n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nL=50\\,\\text{m},\\,W=25\\,\\text{m},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=0.2\\,\\text{m}/\\text{min}.\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe relation between the two time-dependent variables, $V$ and $h,$ is given by the formula for the volume of water:\n\n$$\nV=LWh\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of $V=LWh$ with respect to $t,$ treating both $L$ and $W$ as constants, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}V & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(LWh) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =LW\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can plug in the known values and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =(50)(25)\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =1250\\cdot 0.2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =250.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of water in the aquarium is increasing at a rate of $250\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/h}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
